-- Bug: declaring a view entity's association column as an attribute wrote a
-- model that does not build (ako/view-entity-examples FINDINGS §4).
--
-- `select m.ID as MeterRef` already gives the view entity an association named
-- MeterRef (see view-entity-association.mdl). Declaring it again in the
-- attribute list — `MeterRef: Trends.Meter`, or `MeterRef: Trends.Meter.ID` —
-- parsed, because a bare qualified name is how MDL spells an enumeration type,
-- and exec stored exactly that beside the association.
--
-- Measured on 11.14.0 before the fix, against a module with Trends.Meter,
-- Trends.Reading and Trends.Reading_Meter:
--   check           -> "Check passed!"
--   check -p        -> "OQL select has 1 columns but 2 attributes declared", and
--                      MeterRef compared against the NEXT column ('r.Kwh')
--   exec --no-check -> writes MeterRef: Enumeration(Trends.Meter)
--   mx check        -> CE1613 "The selected enumeration 'Trends.Meter' no longer exists."
--                      With Trends.Meter.ID: mx check throws "An error occurred when
--                      trying to set the 'Enumeration' property of a Enumeration"
--                      and validates nothing.
--
-- Fixed: MDL080 refuses it in check with no project, and exec refuses it before
-- writing, --no-check included (0 files changed). An entity-typed attribute under
-- a name that is NOT an association column needs the project to tell an entity
-- from an enumeration; that refusal is in check -p and exec, and covered by unit
-- tests in mdl/executor/view_attribute_object_test.go.
--
-- Expected: check fails with MDL080.

create view entity Trends.ReadingVE (
  MeterRef: Trends.Meter,
  Kwh: Decimal
) as (
  from Trends.Reading as r
  join r/Trends.Reading_Meter/Trends.Meter as m
  select m.ID as MeterRef, r.Kwh as Kwh
);
